[Music]  6 11 here it is a tuesday morning and it  is time now for our in your business  segment and peter guzman is joining us  from the latin chamber of commerce  we've been talking about business peter  all morning long because of the new  restrictions that are in place  announced by governor sysolak over the  weekend so these 20  these new restrictions this has a big  effect on your restaurants all sorts of  different businesses around town  what are you hearing from the latin  businesses here in southern nevada  about the effect of these new  restrictions  yeah you know uh ironically uh the  response so far has been hey  uh we're glad we're at least being able  to stay open and not completely  uh closed down that's where the real  fear was was a complete closure and so  uh you know they're they're i wouldn't  say excited but  the opportunity to stay open and and  still produce  is uh it keeps them hopeful you know i  think it was the nevada restaurant  association that was saying that maybe  30 percent of the restaurants in town  uh could close by december i acknow we  acknowledge that it's not just  restaurants that are being affected by  these new restrictions here but  how worried are the business leaders  you're talking to  about their survival business-wise  yeah i mean listen we're going through a  pandemic everybody's worried  running a business is uh is a tough  profit margin even before covet so  imagine now it's even more challenging  and with all these restrictions yeah it  becomes challenging but  you're seeing some of these restaurants  pivot uh which is what entrepreneurs do  pivot to outdoor dining uh curbside  more of all of that stuff i think  outdoor dining is gonna become a big  deal  um and part of the business plan and so  again uh  you know not an exciting time but  certainly hopeful that uh they're able  to at least stay open and produce a  little bit of  revenue but it's definitely a challenge  and there's some nervousness  the local latin community the the  hispanic community has been hit is  particularly hard by the coronavirus the  number of cases  and that and those parts of the valley  have are higher than all  other parts adding this as another layer  here  it seems like this is a a pretty  difficult mixture to try and navigate  when you talk to folks in the community  about that what are they telling you  about  their abilities moving forward  well listen you know the the hispanic  culture lends itself to  to uh to be vulnerable on a situation  like this because  families tend to live in bigger groups  together uh you know grandma and grandpa  uh living at home and then you have  living at home so youngsters can bring  that home as you know  uh last month we the latin chamber gave  out over 25  000 reusable masks in trying to  curve that the terrible pandemic that  we're going through  but at the end of the day um we're all  going through it together  there are some things that we can do uh  i believe to  to help uh minimize and that is wearing  the mask and that's why we're  encouraging everybody to  you know wear the mask do their part and  and get tested if you have a symptom  and and let's keep vegas open as much as  we can we're  it seems like we're getting close to a  vaccine so keeping us  open keeps us hopeful when it comes to  final question here to  assistants though a lot of this depends  on the federal government obviously but  what discussions have you had with  lawmakers whether it's clark county  legislators or the governor's office  about some sort of financial assistance  either for businesses to stay open or  maybe just people trying to stay in  their homes  well truly i i do believe that we as  a state and local municipalities have  done an amazing job the county  it just announced another round of help  for small businesses  we've been involved with the tri  chambers uh in giving out grant dollars  the state the the treasury uh  has been working really hard and getting  an additional 20 million dollars  uh in funds for small business we know  that small business is the engine that  runs  uh not only our communities but our  country and so everything we can do to  get dollars in their hands and keep them  open  gives us a fighting chance a long way to  go  hopefully not too much longer but uh the  the hope is there hopefully the money  and the assistance will be as well peter  thank you for joining us again this  morning we know there's a big challenge  ahead for our local businesses we  appreciate it peter good to see you  always a pleasure being with you and  thank you for highlighting things like  this of course

